** expresses impossibility according to the stated evidence.","keywords":["impossibility"],"skill":"impossibility","contextKey":"modal-deduction-49","fingerprint":"a8af47215ecc3b17a3e29a25f0fc35a6097f560624c9dfb316f99c2cf59d46ae","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-deduction"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Choose the modal for an **impossible explanation**, not a prohibition.","**Cannot** expresses impossibility according to the stated evidence.","Choose **cannot**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"May would leave the event possible.","B":"Must not normally prohibits an action rather than ruling it out as impossible.","C":"**Cannot** expresses impossibility according to the stated evidence.","D":"Must would give a strong positive conclusion."}}},{"id":"1684356d-a7da-5b37-a119-aebf310d5a5d","topicSlug":"modal-deduction","level":"B2","prompt":"The evidence is inconclusive; this is only possible: “They ____ be preparing the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"have to"},{"id":"B","text":"might"},{"id":"C","text":"must"},{"id":"D","text":"cannot"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The prompt says **possible but uncertain**.","explanation":"**Might** leaves the action possible without claiming certainty.","keywords":["uncertain-possibility"],"skill":"uncertain-possibility","contextKey":"modal-deduction-49","fingerprint":"afe859f1c8f1cf5bd1e4d347b62e4d56262c7d4650e54b542fd00eb00d52aded","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-deduction"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The prompt says **possible but uncertain**.","**Might** leaves the action possible without claiming certainty.","Choose **might**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Have to ordinarily introduces a requirement here, not the requested uncertain possibility.","B":"**Might** leaves the action possible without claiming certainty.","C":"Must would express a stronger positive conclusion than the prompt supports.","D":"Cannot rules the action out."}}},{"id":"f6c90b96-b4b8-57fd-a41c-af10dcab4b7f","topicSlug":"modal-deduction","level":"B2","prompt":"Build a deduction about an action happening now: “She must ____ the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"been preparing"},{"id":"B","text":"be preparing"},{"id":"C","text":"preparing"},{"id":"D","text":"is preparing"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"A present ongoing deduction has the form **modal + be + -ing**.","explanation":"After must, use **be + preparing** for an ongoing action.","keywords":["deduction-continuous-form"],"skill":"deduction-continuous-form","contextKey":"modal-deduction-49","fingerprint":"5ad6badec7bae5fa4ebf41f21dccf400615a28e029c380fbd5def44b0a481633","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-deduction"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A present ongoing deduction has the form **modal + be + -ing**.","After must, use **be + preparing** for an ongoing action.","Choose **be preparing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Been requires a perfect auxiliary have before it.","B":"After must, use **be + preparing** for an ongoing action.","C":"The auxiliary be is missing.","D":"Must takes base be, not finite is."}}},{"id":"c416587d-f788-59e7-a745-45857ed808dc","topicSlug":"modal-deduction","level":"B2","prompt":"You draw a strong positive conclusion from clear evidence: “They ____ be repairing the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"must not"},{"id":"B","text":"cannot"},{"id":"C","text":"might"},{"id":"D","text":"must"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Identify the intended strength: **strong positive deduction**.","explanation":"Deductive **must** expresses the strong positive conclusion requested.","keywords":["strong-positive-deduction"],"skill":"strong-positive-deduction","contextKey":"modal-deduction-50","fingerprint":"7708e0a3765f53024fcba508265144896e54d1735d1c146ca9094408b00c5641","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-deduction"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Identify the intended strength: **strong positive deduction**.","Deductive **must** expresses the strong positive conclusion requested.","Choose **must**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Must not normally conveys prohibition, not a positive deduction.","B":"Cannot expresses a negative conclusion, the opposite of the prompt.","C":"Might expresses uncertainty rather than the requested strong conclusion.","D":"Deductive **must** expresses the strong positive conclusion requested."}}},{"id":"f6486698-613d-54bb-a68c-f06394eb614d","topicSlug":"modal-deduction","level":"B2","prompt":"The evidence rules this out as impossible: “They ____ be repairing the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"must not"},{"id":"B","text":"cannot"},{"id":"C","text":"must"},{"id":"D","text":"may"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Choose the modal for an **impossible explanation**, not a prohibition.","explanation":"**Cannot** expresses impossibility according to the stated evidence.","keywords":["impossibility"],"skill":"impossibility","contextKey":"modal-deduction-50","fingerprint":"757af594285c9ff1da087c16408f10311cf397ba39a67b23f7f9585eb12216e7","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-deduction"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Choose the modal for an **impossible explanation**, not a prohibition.","**Cannot** expresses impossibility according to the stated evidence.","Choose **cannot**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Must not normally prohibits an action rather than ruling it out as impossible.","B":"**Cannot** expresses impossibility according to the stated evidence.","C":"Must would give a strong positive conclusion.","D":"May would leave the event possible."}}},{"id":"4c9af2ef-c2b5-5bf4-aa61-d673f71824ec","topicSlug":"modal-deduction","level":"B2","prompt":"The evidence is inconclusive; this is only possible: “They ____ be repairing the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"might"},{"id":"B","text":"must"},{"id":"C","text":"cannot"},{"id":"D","text":"have to"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The prompt says **possible but uncertain**.","explanation":"**Might** leaves the action possible without claiming certainty.","keywords":["uncertain-possibility"],"skill":"uncertain-possibility","contextKey":"modal-deduction-50","fingerprint":"67c98f668cce55f6a838cde8c639f5ca942287e6d681f86633dbaff8b6c9acf2","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-deduction"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The prompt says **possible but uncertain**.","**Might** leaves the action possible without claiming certainty.","Choose **might**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Might** leaves the action possible without claiming certainty.","B":"Must would express a stronger positive conclusion than the prompt supports.","C":"Cannot rules the action out.","D":"Have to ordinarily introduces a requirement here, not the requested uncertain possibility."}}},{"id":"fc1ce4b7-ce34-5f94-a7e8-dd42e5b6dd40","topicSlug":"modal-deduction","level":"B2","prompt":"Build a deduction about an action happening now: “She must ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"been repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"be repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"is repairing"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"A present ongoing deduction has the form **modal + be + -ing**.","explanation":"After must, use **be + repairing** for an ongoing action.","keywords":["deduction-continuous-form"],"skill":"deduction-continuous-form","contextKey":"modal-deduction-50","fingerprint":"4f5e4106d67bb6e33a36b09d4dac767fcf0729d1528f2d1d70e4c0b0557c3928","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-deduction"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A present ongoing deduction has the form **modal + be + -ing**.","After must, use **be + repairing** for an ongoing action.","Choose **be repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Been requires a perfect auxiliary have before it.","B":"After must, use **be + repairing** for an ongoing action.","C":"The auxiliary be is missing.","D":"Must takes base be, not finite is."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Express conclusions and possibilities without confusing certainty with obligation.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"A strong positive conclusion","explanation":"Must can express a strong deduction based on evidence. This differs from must as an obligation. Must be working means the speaker concludes that the activity is happening; it does not necessarily order anyone to work.","pattern":"The lights are on; someone **must be working**.","commonMistakes":["A deduction is the speaker’s judgement, not an absolute guarantee."],"examples":["You draw a strong positive conclusion from clear evidence: “They **must** be preparing the visitor guide.”","You draw a strong positive conclusion from clear evidence: “They **must** be restoring the old portrait.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"A strong negative conclusion","explanation":"Can’t and cannot can express that something is impossible according to the available evidence. Mustn’t normally expresses prohibition rather than the ordinary negative counterpart of deductive must.","pattern":"That **can’t be** the right address.","commonMistakes":["Do not substitute mustn’t when you mean that something is impossible."],"examples":["The evidence rules this out as impossible: “They **cannot** be preparing the visitor guide.”","The evidence rules this out as impossible: “They **cannot** be restoring the old portrait.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"An uncertain possibility","explanation":"May, might and could often express an uncertain possibility. Their relative strength depends on context, intonation and variety, so these exercises do not assign fixed percentage probabilities. A negative may not or might not means possibly not.","pattern":"She **might be** at home.","commonMistakes":["Could not often suggests impossibility; it is not always interchangeable with might not."],"examples":["The evidence is inconclusive; this is only possible: “They **might** be preparing the visitor guide.”","The evidence is inconclusive; this is only possible: “They **might** be restoring the old portrait.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Match the verb form to the time","explanation":"For a present activity, use modal + be + -ing. For a state, use modal + base verb. Future probability can also use be likely to or probably. Keep probably before a negative modal in the common phrase probably won’t.","pattern":"They **must be waiting**. / They **are likely to arrive** soon.","commonMistakes":["A present deduction about an activity needs be before -ing; must waiting is incomplete."],"examples":["Build a deduction about an action happening now: “She must **be preparing** the visitor guide.”","Build a deduction about an action happening now: “She must **be restoring** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["The lights are on; someone **must be working**.","That **can’t be** the right address.","She **might be** at home.","They **must be waiting**. / They **are likely to arrive** soon."]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["modal-obligation"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
